Set a volume to error when its create loses the database

The manager's create flow starts with `ExtractVolumeRefTask`, which refreshes
the volume from the database. When the database is gone at that moment, the
task fails and its revert returns at once: a failed task of its own is taken
for a volume that was deleted, and `set_error` is False in this flow. No later
task ran, so nothing ends the create. The volume stays in `creating`, no user
can delete it, and only the next start of cinder-volume cleans it up. A later
task that fails during the same outage gets no further:
`OnFailureRescheduleTask` calls `common.error_out`, whose one save fails too
and is logged away.

Random chaos found this on a local replay of seed 41258545774 with the patch
of nova 0001 in place (issues #1581, #1582 and #1583 report the same three
red checks): s08-mariadb-pod-kill hit a volume between the API's commit and
cinder-volume's refresh. dizzy's teardown failed on the volume with a 400 and
exited 1, and the stuck-resources Job found it in `creating`.

`ExtractVolumeRefTask.revert` now sets the volume to error when its own
failure is a `DBConnectionError`, and `error_out` saves the status with
retries whose waits add up to 254 seconds, which outlasts a database failover.
A `VolumeNotFound` still reverts to nothing, because that volume is gone.

Tests: no upstream test pins either path. The patch adds
VolumeCreateLostDatabaseTestCase to
cinder/tests/unit/volume/flows/test_create_volume_flow.py; two of its three
tests fail without the patch.

Proof: tests/container-images/verify_cinder.sh asserts in the built image that
the revert of a lost database sets the volume to error.

Applies to cinder 28.0.0 (2026.1).

Upstream status: not yet proposed.

